[252.593225, "o", "BUG ANALYSIS AND FIX\r\n====================\r\n\r\nPROBLEM:\r\nThe restore.sh script was failing intermittently with \"gzip: stdin: not in gzip format\" errors.\r\n\r\nROOT CAUSE:\r\nThe .bashrc file contains a locale rotation mechanism that alternates LC_COLLATE between 'C' and 'en_US.UTF-8' on each shell invocation. The original restore.sh used shell glob expansion (chunk_*) to concatenate files, which can produce different ordering depending on locale settings. When chunks are concatenated in the wrong order, the gzip stream becomes corrupted.\r\n\r\nORIGINAL CODE (restore.sh):\r\ncat \"$CHUNKS_DIR\"/chunk_* | gunzip | tar xf - -C \"$OUTPUT_DIR\"\r\n\r\nFIXED CODE (restore.sh):\r\nfind \"$CHUNKS_DIR\" -name 'chunk_*' -type f | sort | xargs cat | gunzip | tar xf - -C \"$OUTPUT_DIR\"\r\n\r\nSOLUTION:\r\nReplaced glob expansion with explicit sorting using find + sort + xargs. This ensures chunks are always concatenated in the correct lexicographic order regardless of locale settings.\r\n\r\nVERIFICATION:\r\n- Tested restore with LC_COLLATE=C: SUCCESS\r\n- T"]
[252.593284, "o", "ested restore with LC_COLLATE=en_US.UTF-8: SUCCESS\r\n- Ran 5 consecutive tests with alternating locales: ALL SUCCESS\r\n- Verified data integrity with md5sum: MATCHES ORIGINAL\r\n\r\nSTATUS: FIXED\r\nBoth archive.sh and restore.sh are now working reliably.\r\n"]
